rules.body:
Separate packet admission, retained history and visible publication. Bind admitted decoding to its originating file and attempt; retain its history effect on completion even after cancellation, hiding, replacement or disposal. Original src/progress.js: "    const deliver = frame => { record(frame); receive(frame); };" README.md: "`recordProgress` appends transfer history. `showProgress` paints the panel." Recheck visit authority after recording because host callbacks may navigate synchronously. Original src/queue.js: "      if (state.current === visit) state.showProgress({ fileId: visit.file.id, attemptId, frame });" Cancellation of an attempt alone does not revoke presentation of already admitted decoding while its visit remains current and its frame remains eligible.

Coordinate cleanup ownership across synchronous startup, listener removal and cancellation. Retire captured ownership before releasing resources; after callbacks return, verify that an older operation cannot overwrite newer navigation. Original src/panel.js: "  const previous = state.current;
  state.current = null;
  if (previous) releaseVisit(previous);
  if (state.disposed || state.nextVisit !== requestId) return null;" Preserve removal commitment and repeated-removal behavior. src/progress-source.js: "// Removal commits before notifying the host. The host callback is synchronous
// and may call workspace.select(...) or workspace.hide(). Repeated removal is inert." Preserve abort, progress cleanup and cancellation notification order from src/transfer.js: "  controller.signal.addEventListener("abort", () => {
    stopProgress();
    onCancel({ file, attemptId, signal: controller.signal });
  }, { once: true });" Reconcile resources returned after retirement without replacing newer ownership.

Keep an accepted retry bound to its entry visit and file across cancellation callbacks. Cancel the previous attempt before enqueueing; preserve the accepted retry's record and transfer construction even if cancellation navigates; cancel the resulting retired handle and suppress its queued notice. Original src/retry.js: "  const previous = visit.handle;
  previous.cancel();
  const handle = enqueueTransfer(state, visit, "retry");
  if (state.current === visit) visit.handle = handle;
  else handle.cancel();
  if (state.current === visit) publishQueued(state, visit, handle);" Preserve queue recording before transfer construction, independent workspace counters and idempotent disposal. src/queue.js: "// Queue records are retained even when their originating panel visit ends."

Replace the retired request with the existing local configuration while retaining the constructor interface. src/config.py: "LOCAL_CONFIG = {
    "fields": ["parent_id", "parent_code", "parent_name"],
    "picker": "unit_lookup",
    "initial_values": {},
}". Original src/record_editor.py: "    def __init__(self, form, load_config):
        self._form = form
        self._load_config = load_config". Removing this request does not authorize removing the constructor parameter or changing the configuration definition.

Preserve configuration application before evaluating and writing the supplied record's parent fields, without inserting another suspension between these operations. Original src/record_editor.py: "        self._form.apply_config(config)
        self._form.write(
            {
                "parent_id": record["parent_id"],
                "parent_code": record["parent_code"],
                "parent_name": record["parent_name"],
            }
        )". Keep these values sourced from record rather than LOCAL_CONFIG.initial_values. This preserves existing evaluation order without assuming undocumented callback behavior.

Update the accepting editor's in-memory text synchronously, then enqueue persistence using the existing trailing-task utility with a 120 ms quiet interval. Bind delayed work to the accepting document rather than consulting the later selection. Preserve editing without a selection as a no-op and allow empty text. src/editor.js contains "if (!current) return;" and "current.text = text;". TASK requires: "Subsequent bursts
must continue to save." The internal ownership structure may vary if these observable requirements hold.

Retire a consumed batch before invoking persistence so nested flushing cannot consume it twice and edits accepted by an inline listener establish independent pending work. Preserve src/trailing-task.js ordering: "    handle = null;
    const args = pending;
    pending = null;
    if (args !== null) work(...args);" Do not add completion cleanup that erases a callback-enqueued batch.

On departure, capture the old editor resources, revoke current ownership, detach its badge, and complete old-view clearing before flushing its pending persistence, or protect any remaining effects against newer navigation. Flush before reading a replacement document. Recheck navigation request authority after callback-capable persistence before installing a destination. README.md states: "Reopening a document restores its saved text in a new editor visit." It also states: "Navigation precedence is
ordered by request start: a request that starts later wins even when an earlier
request returns afterward." src/save-status.js requires that the departing badge "must produce no further output, including during that
// navigation call." Cleanup must use captured departing resources so it cannot detach or erase a successor's resources or edits.

At submission entry, capture the originating visit and its document/text snapshot before persistence can invoke integrations. Persist the accepted text before archive capture, then create and return exactly one receipt even if persistence navigates or accepts further edits. README.md states: "`submit()` accepts the document and latest text selected at its call entry." It further requires: "Accepted text
must be persisted before the archive captures the receipt. Later edits or
navigation cannot change that receipt's document, text, or obligation to exist." Preserve the no-selection null result without creating a receipt.

Replace Personnel's remote request with src/config/personnel-form.json. Preserve asynchronous initialization completion and applicable error reporting: original src/editors/PersonnelEditor.vue contains "return getStaticConfig("personnel-form")" and ".catch((error) => {
          this.loadError = error.message;
        });". Preserve initialization on active mounting and later activation: "mounted() {
    if (this.active) this.getPageConfig();
  },
  watch: {
    active(next) {
      if (next) this.getPageConfig();
    },
  },".

Retain the imperative write of all three parent fields, alongside declarative defaults. Original src/editors/PersonnelEditor.vue contains "this.formApi.setValue({
            parentId: this.parentNode.id,
            parentCode: this.parentNode.code,
            parentName: this.parentNode.name,
          });". src/components/SchemaForm.vue implements "this.values = { ...this.values, ...values };
          this.imperativeWriteCount += 1;" separately from rule hydration's "this.ruleHydrationCount += 1;". Declarative hydration therefore cannot replace the imperative effect. Evaluate parentNode fields when applying configuration after readiness rather than capturing them before the wait.

Keep customization local and preserve application order. Original src/editors/PersonnelEditor.vue begins with "const rules = config.rule.map((item) => ({ ...item }));
      this.option = { ...config.option };". It prepares defaults with "if (item.field === "parentCode") item.value = this.parentNode.code;" and "item.value = this.parentNode.name;", invokes setValue within the parentName branch, and publishes "this.rule = rules;" afterward. Retain these clones, defaults and ordering without mutating the imported JSON or inserting a suspension inside configuration application.

Preserve both picker entry points. src/editors/PersonnelEditor.vue contains "<button type="button" @click="openPicker">Choose unit</button>", "item.on = { search: () => this.openPicker() };", and "openPicker() {
      this.pickerVisible = true;
    },". Retain the unitLookup search callback and button behavior during local configuration customization.

Enqueue the latest accepted text using the existing trailing-task utility with a 120 ms quiet interval, binding delayed work to the accepting document rather than consulting a later selection. Preserve empty text, subsequent bursts, and editing without a selection as a no-op. The original src/editor.js contains "if (current) store.write(current.id, text);"; src/trailing-task.js retains arguments with "pending = args;". These sources do not require an additional in-memory text model or persistent buffer bank.

Retire consumed pending state before invoking callback-capable persistence. Preserve src/trailing-task.js ordering: "    handle = null;
    const args = pending;
    pending = null;
    if (args !== null) work(...args);" This prevents nested flushing from consuming the same batch twice and lets callback-enqueued edits establish independent work. Do not add completion cleanup that erases that work.

Capture departing resources, revoke current ownership, detach the captured badge, and finish old-view clearing before flushing pending persistence, or guard any remaining effects against newer navigation. Flush before reading a replacement document to preserve the original synchronous persistence and restoration behavior. TASK states: "Preserve the workspace's existing navigation and persistence behavior." Original src/editor.js orders departure as "    const leaving = current;
    current = null;
    if (leaving) leaving.stop();
    renderDocument(null);" and replacement rendering uses "renderDocument({ id, visit, text: store.read(id) });". src/save-status.js states: "// badge immediately: it must produce no further output, including during that
// navigation call. Saving the document itself is a separate workspace concern."

After callback-capable departure saving, revalidate the open request before installing its destination and revalidate enclosing workspace navigation before committing or clearing its projection. Protect clearing effects as well as installation. src/draft-store.js directly invokes subscribers with "if (listeners.has(listener)) listener(event);"; src/workspace.js registers "const stop = store.subscribe(event => onSaved(event, navigation));". README.md requires: "The outline and breadcrumbs should continue to describe what the user
is viewing, while the store should retain accepted edits." src/navigation-projection.js states: "// decides whether a requested visit was retained before committing it here." A superseded continuation must not publish a visit that the editor did not retain.

Use the documented service status boundary: src/preparation-api.js states "// transport resolves false while preparation is pending and true when ready." Pending must retain loading without recording completion. Preserve the existing READY branch in src/usePreparation.js: "state.current.loading = false;" and "if (!state.current.completed.includes(requestToken)) {
          state.current.completed.push(requestToken);
        }". Preserve the request-token snapshot "const requestToken = state.current.token;" rather than attributing an old result to a replacement token.

Preserve immediate initial check entry and existing observable state. Original src/usePreparation.js calls "check(beginGeneration());" directly from start and reopen, and performs "state.current.loading = true;
    state.current.checkCount += 1;
    publish();" before transport entry. Keep snapshot copying: "return { ...state.current, completed: [...state.current.completed] };". When retaining retry handles, avoid allowing an obsolete scheduler return or callback to overwrite a replacement run's handle or retryScheduled state. Detaching an owned handle before calling external cancellation and retaining the originating generation across that call are defensive ways to preserve ownership if reentry occurs.

Enqueue the latest accepted text using the existing trailing-task utility with a 120 ms quiet interval. Bind delayed persistence to the document accepting the edit rather than resolving a later selection. Preserve empty text, later bursts, and no-selection editing as a no-op. Original src/editor.js contains "if (current) store.write(current.id, text);"; src/trailing-task.js contains "      pending = args;
      handle = timers.schedule(flush, delay);". These sources do not require a separate in-memory text model or reusable document buffer bank.

Retire consumed pending arguments and timer ownership before invoking callback-capable persistence. Preserve src/trailing-task.js ordering: "    handle = null;
    const args = pending;
    pending = null;
    if (args !== null) work(...args);" Nested flushing must not consume the same batch twice, and completion cleanup must not erase a batch enqueued by a subscriber.

Capture departing resources, revoke current ownership, stop the captured status subscription, and complete old-view clearing before flushing pending persistence, or guard remaining effects against successor navigation. Flush before reading a replacement document. Original src/editor.js contains "    const leaving = current;
    current = null;
    if (leaving) leaving.stop();
    renderDocument(null);" and "      renderDocument({ id, text: store.read(id) });". Together with README.md's "its last accepted edit", the original immediate write supports synchronous departure flushing across switch, same-document reopen, close, and disposal.

Stopping a subscription must also revoke delivery by a callback already captured in an ongoing notification snapshot. src/draft-store.js contains "      for (const listener of [...listeners]) listener(event);" and "      return () => listeners.delete(listener);"; removing Set membership cannot remove a callback from that existing array. README.md requires "they stop when that view begins closing." Retain document filtering from src/save-status.js, "    if (event.id === id) renderStatus(event);", while adding a view-local delivery guard that is revoked before unsubscribing.

After departure saving invokes subscribers, ensure an outer open continuation does not overwrite a successor opened or closed during that save. Protect clearing effects as well as destination installation; captured departing cleanup must not affect successor resources or pending edits. src/draft-store.js establishes the callback boundary with "      records.set(id, text);
      const event = { id, text };
      for (const listener of [...listeners]) listener(event);". A navigation generation check is one implementation strategy for preserving ownership across the newly introduced departure save, not evidence of a universal navigation precedence contract.

Separate admission, retained audit and visible delivery. Capture originating file and attempt identity for admitted decoding. Preserve audit once at completion even after cancellation, retirement or disposal; then check originating visit authority before visible publication. README.md: "Every accepted progress event is recorded exactly once when decoding
finishes, including work accepted before the visit closes." README.md: "Removing a listener does not retract a decode
that already started. Closing or replacing the visit stops visible progress;
new visits remain live." Original src/progress.js: "    const deliver = frame => { record(frame); receive(frame); };" Original src/queue.js: "      if (state.current === visit) state.showProgress({ fileId: visit.file.id, attemptId, frame });" Preserve this visit-based publication check: attempt cancellation alone does not revoke visible delivery of previously admitted decoding while its visit remains current. Reopening the identical file creates a different visit and does not authorize old results to paint it.

Coordinate cleanup ownership before synchronous startup can navigate. Make retirement prevent new admission immediately, reconcile resources returned after retirement, and prevent resumed older work from overwriting newer ownership. README.md: "`showProgress` may synchronously select or hide through
the workspace, and normal current progress must remain visible." Retire captured panel ownership before canceling it, and recheck navigation identity afterward. Original src/panel.js: "  const previous = state.current;
  state.current = null;
  if (previous) releaseVisit(previous);
  if (state.disposed || state.nextVisit !== requestId) return null;" README.md: "Selection and hiding may themselves cancel an old handle and synchronously
receive newer navigation; the newer navigation keeps ownership." Preserve native cancellation and cleanup-before-notification ordering. Original src/transfer.js: "  controller.signal.addEventListener("abort", () => {
    stopProgress();
    onCancel({ file, attemptId, signal: controller.signal });
  }, { once: true });" Preserve idempotence and independent workspace disposal. README.md: "Disposal removes subscriptions and cancels transfers; work already accepted
for audit remains retained."

Bind accepted retries to their entry file and visit across cancellation reentry. Cancel the previous attempt before enqueueing, retain exactly one accepted command and queue record even if cancellation navigates, cancel its resulting transfer if retired, and suppress its retired queued notice. README.md: "The accepted old retry must still enqueue and record once after such navigation.
Its newly created transfer is canceled immediately if its visit has ended; this
does not remove its retained queue record." Original src/retry.js: "  const previous = visit.handle;
  previous.cancel();
  const handle = enqueueTransfer(state, visit, "retry");
  if (state.current === visit) visit.handle = handle;
  else handle.cancel();
  if (state.current === visit) publishQueued(state, visit, handle);" Preserve record-before-transfer construction and independent counters. Original src/queue.js: "  const attemptId = ++state.nextAttempt;
  state.record(makeQueueRecord(visit.file, attemptId, visit.id, kind));"
